          "id": "sec-1-10",
          "secNumber": "1.10",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Molecular Diffusion and Driving Forces",
          "content": "# Unit 1 assessment \u2014 Diffusion\n\nThe assessment distinguishes memorization, calculation, interpretation and model criticism. Use SI conversions explicitly, give dimensional units for computed transfer rates and justify equilibrium assumptions.\n\n## Problem 1: Fick\u2019s first law and sign conventions\n\nA membrane test yields a larger flux when the film is made thinner at otherwise identical driving concentration. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `N_A=-D_AB dc_A/dz`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Separate diffusive and total molar flux before you use a measured permeation rate.\n\n## Problem 2: Diffusivity in gases, liquids and porous solids\n\nAn engineer chooses reference properties from an actual temperature and composition range rather than a single handbook value. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `D_AB(T,P) is material and state dependent`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Do not extrapolate a gas correlation to concentrated electrolytes.\n\n## Problem 3: Steady diffusion through stagnant films\n\nA stagnant solvent film can limit extraction even if the adjacent fluid is vigorously mixed. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `N_A=D_AB(c_A1-c_A2)/L`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Account for Stefan flow if component A diffuses through stagnant B in a gas.\n\n## Problem 4: Unsteady diffusion into a semi-infinite medium\n\nA polymer slab initially free of solvent experiences a surface step and a spreading front. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `(c-c0)/(cs-c0)=erfc(z/(2sqrt(Dt)))`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Semi-infinite conditions fail once the front reaches the back face.\n\n## Problem 5: Laboratory analysis\n\nSelect Laboratory 01 from the unit and export a sensitivity CSV. Report the largest and smallest finite result, calculate a percentage sensitivity at the baseline using two nearby points, and explain whether you expect the same sensitivity when the temperature or composition changes. Give at least two plausible sources of nonrepeatability in a physical test.\n\n**Rubric (100 points):** physical model and assumptions 25; calculations and dimensional checks 25; clear diagrams and evidence 15; data interpretation 20; limitations and safety 15. Numbers produced by the simulation are not independent evidence that its selected correlation applies to a real installation."
