          "id": "sec-2-5",
          "secNumber": "2.5",
          "heading": "Unit 2, Lesson 4: Heat\u2013mass transfer analogies and limitations",
          "content": "# Unit 2, Lesson 4: Heat\u2013mass transfer analogies and limitations\n\n**Unit:** Convective transfer. **Core model:** `j_D=Sh/(Re Sc^(1/3))`. **Interactive laboratory:** [Laboratory 05](../labs/lab-05.html).\n\n## What the phenomenon means\n\nAnalogies can translate transport behavior between temperature and concentration when boundary conditions and transport mechanisms are comparable. The governing relationship is `j_D=Sh/(Re Sc^(1/3))`. In the calculation, every coefficient and concentration must be tied to a specific thermodynamic or hydraulic state. When a textbook draws a flux arrow, that arrow represents a signed rate through a stated area; it does not by itself prove that the assumed regime exists. The objective of the lesson is to connect the measurable laboratory signal to the correct transfer mechanism and to identify when one mechanism is no longer dominant.\n\nConvective transfer coefficients connect a driving concentration difference to a net flux through a near-wall region. Their numerical value depends on length scale and boundary conditions; a coefficient measured around a sphere cannot be carried unchanged to a plate or pipe. The Schmidt number compares momentum and molecular species diffusivity, so using the same liquid viscosity at all temperatures can misstate transfer trends.\n\n## A process example and a defensible workflow\n\nA heat-transfer correlation guides an initial mass-transfer estimate for a dilute gas stream but requires validation. Start with a physical sketch showing control volumes, phase boundaries, sensor positions and the relevant flow rates. Identify the driving potential, compute a result on a consistent mole, mass, time and area basis, and translate the result into a quantity that a laboratory could measure. Confirm conservation independently. A predicted high flux is not automatically useful: it may require an unacceptable pressure, cause overheating, or imply an unrealistic solvent inventory. The preferred answer balances transfer rate with credible operating limits.\n\nBefore a design estimate becomes a purchasing specification, attach a measurement plan: the temperature and composition range, the reference for transport properties, the proposed independent validation case and an acceptable error tolerance. If the coefficient comes from a chart, record the relevant fluid regime, dimensionless groups and length definition so that another engineer can reproduce it.\n\n## Interpretation and validity\n\nThe most valuable experimental observable is often the slope of mass released or absorbed versus time under carefully controlled bulk flow. Measure velocity, density and viscosity to identify Reynolds number. A proposed Sherwood correlation should be tested only across its reported Reynolds, Schmidt and geometry range. If the surface reaction is fast, its apparent concentration may approximate zero; if the surface reaction is slow, fluid-side transfer may not control.\n\nA reliable material balance begins by choosing a control surface and defining what passes through it. If the measured concentration belongs to the bulk while the governing law requires the interfacial value, the missing boundary condition must be solved or independently measured. Draw the transfer path before deciding which concentration difference is available. This small discipline prevents fitting a physically impossible flux to a convenient linear graph.\n\n**Failure mode to recognize:** Large blowing/suction, variable properties and phase change can defeat analogies. Document at least one observation that would falsify the current model. If a theoretical response is monotonic, ask what physical effect could make an observed curve flatten or reverse. The simulation provides a controlled numerical experiment; only measured property data and an appropriate scale-up study can establish real equipment performance.\n\n## Guided investigation\n\n- Write each symbol and its SI units for `j_D=Sh/(Re Sc^(1/3))`. Distinguish a total rate from a flux and any concentration ratio from a dimensional concentration.\n- Run the linked laboratory at its baseline settings and export the parameter-response curve. Identify a portion where the physical response is strongest.\n- Predict the effect of raising the principal independent parameter by 10%, then compare the simulated value with your prediction. Explain any nonlinear response using the original equation.\n- Identify a measurement that would estimate the most uncertain coefficient. Describe sensor position, replicate count and whether a control experiment is needed.\n\n## Questions for self-check\n\n1. Which assumption permits the governing equation to be used here, and what real observation might contradict it?\n2. If the primary driving-force measurement has a +5% systematic bias, what direction of bias would appear in the inferred coefficient?\n3. Which is more useful for scaling this model: matching the total mass-transfer rate or matching the dimensionless groups, and why?"
