          "id": "sec-2-8",
          "secNumber": "2.8",
          "heading": "Experimental Laboratory Investigation: Laboratory 04: Porous-film effective diffusivity",
          "content": "# Laboratory 04: Porous-film effective diffusivity\n\n**Relevant unit:** Diffusion. **Governing relationship:** `D_eff = \u03b5D/\u03c4; N=D_eff \u0394c/L`.\n\n## Physical question\n\nWhat happens to the predicted porous-film effective diffusivity when its principal adjustable parameter changes, and how does that sensitivity relate to the mechanism described in this unit? The baseline parameter values are synthetic teaching inputs, not measurements from a named plant. This experiment is designed to expose a mathematical trend and make its units visible before real property data are substituted.\n\n## Experimental setup and protocol\n\nPorosity\u2013tortuosity idealization without sorption. List the assumptions on paper before changing inputs. Treat the first control as the independent variable; keep the remaining parameters fixed for the first sweep. Read the numerical output at the minimum, baseline and maximum setting, then export the 121-point curve. Repeat at a second value of the parameter most strongly coupled to the driving force. Use the on-screen schematic to identify the direction of transport and the conceptual location of the limiting resistance. It is not a scale drawing.\n\n## Parameters\n\n- **Porosity:** default `0.4 fraction`, adjustable range `0.1\u20130.8 fraction`. Check the measurement source and correlation domain before substitution.\n- **Tortuosity:** default `2 \u2014`, adjustable range `1\u20135 \u2014`. Check the measurement source and correlation domain before substitution.\n- **Molecular diffusivity:** default `1e-09 m\u00b2/s`, adjustable range `1e-10\u20134e-09 m\u00b2/s`. Check the measurement source and correlation domain before substitution.\n- **Concentration difference:** default `60 mol/m\u00b3`, adjustable range `2\u2013120 mol/m\u00b3`. Check the measurement source and correlation domain before substitution.\n- **Thickness:** default `0.01 m`, adjustable range `0.001\u20130.06 m`. Check the measurement source and correlation domain before substitution.\n\n## Interpretation and model boundaries\n\nDiffusion is caused by microscopic motion even when the bulk fluid is nominally stationary. The engineer must identify whether flux refers to a fixed plane, a moving mixture, or an individual component relative to the molar-average velocity. These distinctions are not cosmetic: in an evaporating gas film a nonzero total molar flow adds a convective term to the component flux. In porous materials the accessible cross-sectional area is smaller than the specimen envelope and tortuous paths make the effective diffusion coefficient less than the free-fluid coefficient.\n\nThe response curve is a local sensitivity plot in which other controls remain fixed. It does not automatically represent a physical trajectory through a reactor or a separation column. A reported ''outside model validity'' result indicates a mathematical or physical constraint such as an inverted concentration difference, invalid pinch, an incompatible moisture ordering, or a correlation beyond its specified regime. The laboratory deliberately leaves these cases visible so they can be diagnosed instead of silently clipped.\n\n## Investigation questions\n\n1. Calculate the baseline result independently using `D_eff = \u03b5D/\u03c4; N=D_eff \u0394c/L`. Convert all parameter units first. What is the largest source of numerical uncertainty?\n2. Explain the curvature or straightness of the sweep. Does doubling the first parameter double, halve, or otherwise change the response?\n3. Which plausible operating effect is excluded by the model: property variation, pressure drop, fouling, chemical reaction, unsteady accumulation, or imperfect contact? Describe a test for that effect.\n4. If a customer specification required the output to improve by 15%, identify two possible changes and discuss an operational cost or safety implication of each.\n\n**Use restriction:** Educational sensitivity model; it is not a substitute for verified correlations, material data, equipment design standards or engineering sign-off."
