          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Convective Mass Transfer and Boundary Layers",
          "content": "# Unit 2 assessment \u2014 Convective transfer\n\nThe assessment distinguishes memorization, calculation, interpretation and model criticism. Use SI conversions explicitly, give dimensional units for computed transfer rates and justify equilibrium assumptions.\n\n## Problem 1: Concentration boundary layers and interfacial resistance\n\nA dissolution vessel at fixed stirring speed is tested with several particle sizes to distinguish interfacial control from chemistry. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `N_A=k_c(c_b-c_s)`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Do not infer a coefficient from bulk concentration alone without estimating surface concentration.\n\n## Problem 2: Dimensionless transport groups\n\nA change in solvent viscosity can alter both Re and Sc, so a velocity-only extrapolation is weak. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `Re=rho*u*L/mu; Sc=mu/(rho*D); Sh=k_c*L/D`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Check laminar or turbulent applicability before using a correlation.\n\n## Problem 3: Flat-plate and pipe correlations\n\nA plate-coating dryer is scaled by adjusting air speed and checking whether the flow regime remains comparable. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `Sh_L=0.664 Re_L^0.5 Sc^(1/3) (laminar plate)`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Entrance effects, roughness and chemical reaction invalidate simplified correlations.\n\n## Problem 4: Heat\u2013mass transfer analogies and limitations\n\nA heat-transfer correlation guides an initial mass-transfer estimate for a dilute gas stream but requires validation. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `j_D=Sh/(Re Sc^(1/3))`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Large blowing/suction, variable properties and phase change can defeat analogies.\n\n## Problem 5: Laboratory analysis\n\nSelect Laboratory 05 from the unit and export a sensitivity CSV. Report the largest and smallest finite result, calculate a percentage sensitivity at the baseline using two nearby points, and explain whether you expect the same sensitivity when the temperature or composition changes. Give at least two plausible sources of nonrepeatability in a physical test.\n\n**Rubric (100 points):** physical model and assumptions 25; calculations and dimensional checks 25; clear diagrams and evidence 15; data interpretation 20; limitations and safety 15. Numbers produced by the simulation are not independent evidence that its selected correlation applies to a real installation."
