          "id": "sec-3-9",
          "secNumber": "3.9",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Interphase Mass Transfer and Two-Film Theory",
          "content": "# Unit 3 assessment \u2014 Interphase transfer\n\nThe assessment distinguishes memorization, calculation, interpretation and model criticism. Use SI conversions explicitly, give dimensional units for computed transfer rates and justify equilibrium assumptions.\n\n## Problem 1: Partition equilibria and driving-force conventions\n\nA gas absorber operates near equilibrium at the rich-liquid end, leaving little transfer driving force. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `y_A^*=m x_A`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Use the local interfacial equilibrium, not the global bulk compositions.\n\n## Problem 2: Two-film theory and interfacial states\n\nA solvent with low liquid-side mixing gives poor absorption despite an effective gas-side contactor. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `1/K_G=1/k_G + m/k_L`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Interfacial chemical reaction can fundamentally change the liquid-film model.\n\n## Problem 3: Overall coefficients and controlling resistance\n\nAdding gas agitation to a system controlled by liquid film resistance makes limited difference. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `f_L=(m/k_L)/(1/k_G+m/k_L)`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Coefficients reported for partial pressure and mole fraction cannot be interchanged without conversion.\n\n## Problem 4: Interfacial area and contact equipment\n\nA packed bed flooding event increases liquid holdup but may reduce effective wetting and stable performance. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `r_A=K_G a (y-y^*)`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Do not interpret laboratory kLa as pure diffusivity.\n\n## Problem 5: Laboratory analysis\n\nSelect Laboratory 07 from the unit and export a sensitivity CSV. Report the largest and smallest finite result, calculate a percentage sensitivity at the baseline using two nearby points, and explain whether you expect the same sensitivity when the temperature or composition changes. Give at least two plausible sources of nonrepeatability in a physical test.\n\n**Rubric (100 points):** physical model and assumptions 25; calculations and dimensional checks 25; clear diagrams and evidence 15; data interpretation 20; limitations and safety 15. Numbers produced by the simulation are not independent evidence that its selected correlation applies to a real installation."
