          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Evaporation, Humidification and Industrial Drying",
          "content": "# Unit 5 assessment \u2014 Evaporation and drying\n\nThe assessment distinguishes memorization, calculation, interpretation and model criticism. Use SI conversions explicitly, give dimensional units for computed transfer rates and justify equilibrium assumptions.\n\n## Problem 1: Humid-air state and evaporation potential\n\nHot gas with high relative humidity can be a poor dryer despite an apparently high temperature. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `Y=0.622 p_w/(P-p_w)`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Check saturated vapor-pressure data for the relevant temperature and pressure.\n\n## Problem 2: Evaporators and vaporization duty\n\nA concentrated liquor requires steam above the pure-solvent boiling point. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `Q=m_feed cp(T_b-T_in)+m_vap lambda`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Heat recovery, nonideal enthalpy and boiling-point elevation matter for real designs.\n\n## Problem 3: Constant-rate drying and the critical moisture point\n\nA wet granular product dries almost linearly in time until the critical moisture content. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `t_c=M_dry(X0-Xc)/(A N_c)`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: A measured critical moisture is material and equipment dependent.\n\n## Problem 4: Falling-rate drying and internal diffusion\n\nA food powder reaches a long drying tail that cannot be eliminated by increasing air speed alone. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `t_f=M_dry(Xc-Xe)/(A N_c) ln[(Xc-Xe)/(Xf-Xe)]`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Empirical falling-rate laws must be checked against actual drying curves.\n\n## Problem 5: Laboratory analysis\n\nSelect Laboratory 11 from the unit and export a sensitivity CSV. Report the largest and smallest finite result, calculate a percentage sensitivity at the baseline using two nearby points, and explain whether you expect the same sensitivity when the temperature or composition changes. Give at least two plausible sources of nonrepeatability in a physical test.\n\n**Rubric (100 points):** physical model and assumptions 25; calculations and dimensional checks 25; clear diagrams and evidence 15; data interpretation 20; limitations and safety 15. Numbers produced by the simulation are not independent evidence that its selected correlation applies to a real installation."
