          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Membrane Transport and Selective Permeation",
          "content": "# Unit 6 assessment \u2014 Membrane transport\n\nThe assessment distinguishes memorization, calculation, interpretation and model criticism. Use SI conversions explicitly, give dimensional units for computed transfer rates and justify equilibrium assumptions.\n\n## Problem 1: Membrane permeance and driving pressure\n\nPressure increases membrane productivity until concentration polarization and fouling undermine simple proportionality. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `J_v=L_p(DeltaP-DeltaPi)`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Ignore compaction only in a narrow operating range.\n\n## Problem 2: Selectivity and solute rejection\n\nA nanofiltration membrane rejects a charged solute differently as ionic strength varies. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `R=1-c_p/c_f`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Differentiate observed rejection at the feed from intrinsic rejection at the membrane surface.\n\n## Problem 3: Concentration polarization and boundary layers\n\nA high crossflow velocity increases k and may reduce polarization at a given flux. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `c_m/c_b=exp(J_v/k) for complete rejection film model`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Units of J_v and k must both be length per time.\n\n## Problem 4: Membrane systems, area and staging\n\nMultiple modules operating in series see changing feed composition and pressure. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `Q_p=J_v A`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: A constant-flux calculation is insufficient for high recovery systems.\n\n## Problem 5: Laboratory analysis\n\nSelect Laboratory 14 from the unit and export a sensitivity CSV. Report the largest and smallest finite result, calculate a percentage sensitivity at the baseline using two nearby points, and explain whether you expect the same sensitivity when the temperature or composition changes. Give at least two plausible sources of nonrepeatability in a physical test.\n\n**Rubric (100 points):** physical model and assumptions 25; calculations and dimensional checks 25; clear diagrams and evidence 15; data interpretation 20; limitations and safety 15. Numbers produced by the simulation are not independent evidence that its selected correlation applies to a real installation."
