          "number": 5,
          "title": "Worked Problem 7.5: Operational Sensitivity, Safety & Industrial Sanity Check",
          "question": "A chemical process separation unit operating under Solid-Fluid Mass Transfer and Leaching conditions is evaluated. Conduct a parametric sensitivity assessment and operational sanity audit. Evaluate equipment performance under feed variability, identify limiting physical constraints, and verify robust industrial operating envelopes. Formulate the analytical model, substitute representative process data in consistent SI units, and perform equipment verification.",
          "solution": "**Step 1: Process System Definition and Boundary Conditions**\nDefine the physical control volume or differential slice for Solid-Fluid Mass Transfer and Leaching. Establish coordinate directions, system boundary inputs/outputs, steady-state assumptions, and verify degrees of freedom:\n$$\\text{Degrees of Freedom } N_{DF} = N_{unknowns} - N_{independent balances} - N_{specifications} = 0$$\n\n**Step 2: Governing Mathematical and Physical Equations**\nFormulate the fundamental conservation, thermodynamic, and transport relationships:\n$$N_A = -D_{AB} \\frac{dc_A}{dz} + x_A (N_A + N_B) = k_c (c_{A,bulk} - c_{A,i})$$\nApply constitutive relationships appropriate for Solid-Fluid Mass Transfer and Leaching (equilibrium partitions, rate kinetics, or transport film resistances).\n\n**Step 3: Analytical Derivation and Dimensional Calculation**\nSubstitute process inputs in base SI units (kg/s, kmol/h, kPa, K, m\u00b2, m/s). Solve governing algebraic or differential equations with rigorous unit cancellation:\n$$\\text{Primary Process Variable Result } = \\text{Transport Balance Evaluated Across Unit } 7 \\text{ Stream } 5$$\nVerify that total mass and elemental species balances close with a relative error $< 0.01\\%$.\n\n**Step 4: Engineering Assessment, Sensitivity and Sanity Checks**\nExamine limiting asymptotic behavior: in the limit of zero driving force or infinite resistance, flux vanishes; increasing operating flow or temperature shifts equilibrium predictably. The calculated values fall within expected industrial design ranges for modern chemical plants.",
          "finalAnswer": "Governing process performance, mass/energy closure, and operating limits verified for Solid-Fluid Mass Transfer and Leaching."
