          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Equipment Scaling, Hydrodynamics and Dispersion",
          "content": "# Unit 8 assessment \u2014 Equipment scaling\n\nThe assessment distinguishes memorization, calculation, interpretation and model criticism. Use SI conversions explicitly, give dimensional units for computed transfer rates and justify equilibrium assumptions.\n\n## Problem 1: Interfacial area and geometric scaling\n\nA larger bioreactor with coarse bubbles may lose kLa even at comparable superficial gas speed. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `a=6 epsilon_g/d_b for spherical bubbles`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Foam, coalescence and rheology change the bubble population.\n\n## Problem 2: Residence-time distribution and axial dispersion\n\nA tracer pulse reveals channeling that a mean residence-time measurement cannot detect. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `Pe=u L/D_ax; sigma_theta^2\u22482/Pe for high Pe`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: The high-Pe variance approximation fails in highly dispersed systems.\n\n## Problem 3: Estimating coefficients from experiments\n\nA dissolved oxygen step test estimates kLa only when oxygen uptake and probe response are accounted for. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `ln[(c*-c)/(c*-c0)]=-kLa t`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: Regression uncertainty is not the same as model-structure uncertainty.\n\n## Problem 4: Scale-up decisions, uncertainty and validation\n\nPilot trials should intentionally vary the parameters most likely to break the chosen correlation. Write a component balance and model the transfer using `Report estimated coefficients with applicability range and confidence interval`. Repeat the result after a 20% change in the dominant physical coefficient, if the chosen model is linear in that coefficient. Explain what physically changes when the input varies, specify one observation that would invalidate the equation, and identify the minimum additional measurement required to calculate an engineering uncertainty interval.\n\n**Instructor guidance:** A complete response identifies units and basis, draws the interfacial or control-volume boundary, makes the rate/flux distinction, and explains why a favorable model result may still be infeasible. The reported model limitation should include: A small pilot with uncontrolled chemistry cannot validate an industrial scale-up.\n\n## Problem 5: Laboratory analysis\n\nSelect Laboratory 18 from the unit and export a sensitivity CSV. Report the largest and smallest finite result, calculate a percentage sensitivity at the baseline using two nearby points, and explain whether you expect the same sensitivity when the temperature or composition changes. Give at least two plausible sources of nonrepeatability in a physical test.\n\n**Rubric (100 points):** physical model and assumptions 25; calculations and dimensional checks 25; clear diagrams and evidence 15; data interpretation 20; limitations and safety 15. Numbers produced by the simulation are not independent evidence that its selected correlation applies to a real installation."
